# agentsmemory — configuration for the self-hosted (--local) Docker Compose stack
# (docker-compose.yml). Copy to .env.docker and edit:   cp .env.docker.example .env.docker
#
# This file is short on purpose: local mode has no dashboard, no OAuth handshake
# and no billing — so nothing in .env.example applies here (there is no session
# cookie to sign, no OAuth token to seal, and no API key to reveal — there is no
# API key at all). If you run HOSTED mode instead (docker-compose.prod.yml), the
# container reads .env.prod with the full .env.example set — the Billing block
# at the end of this file documents the OpenCollective variables that apply
# there.
#
# Compose passes these to the container. The one variable it reads for ITSELF
# (AGENTSMEMORY_HOST_PORT, the published host port) must live in your shell or in
# a .env file beside docker-compose.yml — not here.

# --- Reaching the server from another machine ------------------------------
# By default the stack publishes on 127.0.0.1 only (see the ports: block in
# docker-compose.yml), so /mcp is reachable from this host and nowhere else —
# which is what makes an unauthenticated endpoint safe.
#
# To serve other machines on your network, you must do BOTH of these, in this
# order. Widening the publish without setting a token hands every memory in the
# database to anyone on the network:
#   1. Set a token here. The server reads this variable directly, so no change to
#      the command: in docker-compose.yml is needed.
#   2. Widen the publish in docker-compose.yml, e.g. "8080:8080" instead of
#      "127.0.0.1:${AGENTSMEMORY_HOST_PORT:-8080}:8080".
#
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
# AGENTSMEMORY_LOCAL_TOKEN=
#
# Agents then register with the token — the same variable name, so exporting it
# once in your shell configures the client side too:
#   aiagentmemory install --local --token "$AGENTSMEMORY_LOCAL_TOKEN" \
#     --mcp-url http://192.168.1.50:8080/mcp

# --- Embeddings: Ollama --------------------------------------------------
# Compose does not run Ollama. Point at wherever yours already is.
#
# Ollama on the host (macOS, Windows, Linux — the compose file maps this name on
# Linux too):
OLLAMA_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434
#
# Ollama on another machine — a workstation with a GPU, a home server:
# OLLAMA_URL=http://192.168.1.50:11434
# OLLAMA_URL=https://ollama.internal.example.com
#
# TWO THINGS THAT BITE, both on the Ollama side rather than here:
#   1. Ollama binds 127.0.0.1 by default, which a container cannot reach. Start
#      it with OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0 (on macOS: launchctl setenv OLLAMA_HOST
#      0.0.0.0, then restart Ollama; as a systemd service: an override with
#      Environment="OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0").
#   2. The embedding model must be pulled before first use:  ollama pull bge-m3
OLLAMA_EMBED_MODEL=bge-m3

# --- `agentsmemory eval` question generator -------------------------------
# Used ONLY by the eval command; the running server never calls this.
#
# eval writes its own questions — for each sampled drawer a model is asked what
# question that drawer answers — so this model must be GENERATIVE. bge-m3 is an
# embedder and cannot answer /api/generate, so an Ollama holding only embedders
# fails every case with `model '...' not found`:  ollama pull qwen2.5-coder:7b
# EVAL_GEN_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:7b
#
# Defaults to OLLAMA_URL. Set it to send this one-off burst of LLM work to a
# bigger or hosted model while the vectors stay put. Ollama's own /api/generate
# format, so another Ollama (local, remote or hosted) — NOT an OpenAI-shaped
# /v1/chat/completions endpoint.
# EVAL_GEN_URL=https://ollama.com
# EVAL_GEN_API_KEY=

# --- Vector search: chromem (embedded) ------------------------------------
# chromem is a vector database that runs INSIDE the server process: no second
# container, no network hop, no port. It keeps the vectors in memory and persists
# them to /data/agentsmemory.chromem — the same volume as the database, so one
# backup covers both.
#
# SQLite stays the source of truth; this index is derived, and the server refills
# it from SQLite on boot whenever it is empty. Deleting the .chromem directory is
# therefore a safe way to force a clean rebuild — nothing is re-embedded.
#
# This is also what --local picks on its own; setting it here says it out loud.
VECTOR_BACKEND=chromem

# Two alternatives, both a one-line change:
#   VECTOR_BACKEND=sqlite  — no index at all, the source of truth answers
#     searches itself by scanning. Least memory, slowest queries.
#   VECTOR_BACKEND=qdrant  — a separate Qdrant service, worth it for a large
#     palace or one shared by several machines. The full-stack overlay brings one
#     up and sets these for you (docker-compose.full.yml); set them by hand only
#     if you run Qdrant yourself:
# QDRANT_URL=http://qdrant:6333
# QDRANT_API_KEY=

# --- Reranking (optional) ---------------------------------------------------
# A cross-encoder rescores the best candidates of every search. The embedder
# scores a drawer against a query it never saw; a cross-encoder reads the pair
# together, which is a different and much sharper judgement — bge-m3 and
# bge-reranker-v2-m3 are a matched pair, and this is the second half.
#
# Empty (the default) means search ends at the hybrid vector+BM25 fusion, exactly
# as before. When set, a reranker that is DOWN costs ranking quality and nothing
# else: search falls back to the fused order rather than failing.
#
# The full-stack overlay sets these to a llama.cpp server it runs for you:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.full.yml up -d
#
# ⚠ UNDER THAT OVERLAY THIS FILE DOES NOT DECIDE. RERANK_URL is set in the
# overlay's `environment:` block, which beats `env_file:` — so setting it empty
# here turns nothing off. The overlay reads it from Compose interpolation
# instead, which means your SHELL or a `.env` beside the compose files:
#   RERANK_URL= docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.full.yml up -d
# Worth knowing before you reach for it: on a CPU-only host, 10 documents of
# 1600 chars took 17.25s against the shipped RERANK_TIMEOUT=10s, so every search
# paid the full timeout and fell open to the fused order — same scores to four
# decimals, same ordering, 11.6s per query (#154).
#
# To point at your own instead — a bare host gets /rerank appended, so this works
# for text-embeddings-inference as-is, and llama.cpp needs its explicit path:
# RERANK_URL=http://reranker:8080/v1/rerank
#
# How many fused candidates get cross-encoded. There is deliberately no
# RERANK_MODEL: TEI's /rerank carries no model field, because the model is fixed
# when the server starts (--model-id), so a knob the wire cannot carry would only
# mislead. This file previously offered both RERANK_MODEL and RERANK_TOP_K and
# the server read neither.
# RERANK_POOL=10

# --- Logging --------------------------------------------------------------
# true = per-request HTTP access logs + every SQL statement. Useful the first
# time an agent connects and you want proof the calls are landing.
APP_DEBUG=false

# --- Billing (hosted mode only; ignored by --local) -------------------------
# Local mode has no billing, so the variables below do nothing here. They matter
# for hosted deployments (docker-compose.prod.yml), where the full .env.example
# applies and the Pro "checkout" is a hosted OpenCollective contribution page:
# set BILLING_PROVIDER=opencollective and the URLs below. OpenCollective does not
# SIGN its webhooks, so plans are activated by polling its authenticated GraphQL API
# instead (ADR-042): set OPENCOLLECTIVE_PERSONAL_TOKEN and _COLLECTIVE_SLUG and a
# paid contribution activates within one interval. Leave the token unset and nothing
# polls — activation is then the operator's:
#   agentsmemory set-plan --slug <workspace> --plan pro_monthly
# See .env.example for the full block.
# BILLING_PROVIDER=opencollective
# OPENCOLLECTIVE_CHECKOUT_PRO_MONTHLY=https://opencollective.com/it-uoga/projects/ai-agents-memory/contribute/pro-monthly-104934/checkout
# OPENCOLLECTIVE_CHECKOUT_PRO_ANNUAL=https://opencollective.com/it-uoga/projects/ai-agents-memory/contribute/pro-yearly-104935/checkout
# OPENCOLLECTIVE_PROJECT_URL=https://opencollective.com/it-uoga/projects/ai-agents-memory
# OPENCOLLECTIVE_PERSONAL_TOKEN=oc_pat_replace_me
# OPENCOLLECTIVE_COLLECTIVE_SLUG=ai-agents-memory
# OPENCOLLECTIVE_API_URL=https://api.opencollective.com/graphql/v2
# OPENCOLLECTIVE_RECONCILE_INTERVAL=15m

# --- Ranking knobs -----------------------------------------------------------
# Evidence sent to the cross-encoder. lexical is the zero-latency control;
# semantic embeds overlapping windows from each reassembled long memory and
# selects several distant matches before one cross-encoder call. It is inert
# unless RERANK_URL is set. Restart, then confirm `am_status.ranking` reports
# evidence=lexical or evidence=semantic. Ranking is always per logical memory.
MEMORY_EVIDENCE_SELECTOR=lexical
#
# How a raw cross-encoder score is scaled before RERANK_WEIGHT blends it with the
# fused score.
#   sigmoid  (default) 1/(1+e^-x) on the logit, fused scaled by the pool max.
#            Preserves CONFIDENCE: an indifferent cross-encoder lands flat near
#            0.5 and the lexical evidence decides.
#   minmax   the original. SCALE-FREE on both axes, so a 0.001 logit spread and a
#            10.0 one both span the full range — and on a small pool at weight
#            0.5 an opposed pair TIES and the cross-encoder is discarded outright.
#   rank     position only. No amplification, no confidence, still ties.
# Confirm with `am_status.ranking`, which reports norm=<value>.
RERANK_NORM=sigmoid
#
# Lexical fusion weight: 'auto' (default) scales BM25 per query by how much
# lexical signal the query carries against the candidates; a fixed 0..1 pins it.
# BM25_WEIGHT=auto
#
# 'auto-idf' is 'auto' with each query term weighted by how much it
# discriminates, instead of counted once. The binary count reads a term
# appearing in nearly every candidate as lexical signal — which is exactly when
# BM25 is noise — and auto-idf is ahead on every table measured so far (four,
# across two unrelated corpora). It is not the default only because a ranking
# default changes what every existing palace returns.
# BM25_WEIGHT=auto-idf
#
# Closet curation prior, 0..1. Closets summarise mined sources, and their boost
# promotes drawers from sources a search's closet hits point at. On a curated
# palace that prior helps; on a corpus dominated by raw mined transcripts the
# eval measured the same boost DEMOTING correct answers (~0.10 MRR) — the
# closets cover the curated slice and lift it over the mined gold. Set 0 to
# turn the prior (and its per-search closet lookup) off. 0 is the DEFAULT since
# 2026-08-21; set 1 to restore the full curation prior.
# CLOSET_BOOST=0
#
# Floor on how many distinct memories Search retrieves before ranking,
# independent of the page --limit. 0 (the default) uses the formula: limit×3,
# raised to RERANK_POOL when a cross-encoder will actually run. A positive
# value widens; it never shrinks below that formula and never changes the
# page size. The eval's `production (Search) retrieve-k=50` arm measures this
# floor at the default page of 5.
# RETRIEVE_K=0
#
# How vector and lexical evidence combine: 'rrf' (DEFAULT since 2026-08-21 —
# reciprocal-rank fusion) or 'linear' (blends the two scores, weighted by
# BM25_WEIGHT). Under rrf, BM25_WEIGHT and LEX_NORM are both inert. A linear blend lets one bad
# signal drag a good candidate down; on a large diverse palace BM25 measured
# BELOW vector alone, and since the cross-encoder's pool is taken off the fused
# head, the reranker never saw what fusion had buried. RRF was the best arm
# there. Run `agentsmemory eval` on your own corpus before switching — the rrf
# arm in its table is exactly this question.
# FUSION=rrf
#
# How raw lexical scores are normalised before they are fused: 'page-max'
# (default), 'ceiling' or 'saturating'. page-max scales the page so its best
# lexical match reads 1.0, which means the lexical channel always speaks at full
# volume even when nothing in the page is a good lexical match. The anchored
# transforms measure each candidate against the score the QUERY could have
# attained instead, so a page with no good lexical hit lets the vector channel
# decide. DOES NOTHING when FUSION=rrf: rank fusion combines positions, not
# magnitudes, so there is no lexical magnitude to normalise. The eval's anchored
# arms measure exactly this choice — run it on your own corpus before changing.
# LEX_NORM=page-max


# --- Embeddings ---------------------------------------------------------------
# What turns text into vectors: 'ollama' (default) or 'tei'.
#
# Ollama's embedding API returns DENSE vectors only. bge-m3 natively produces
# three representations — dense, learned sparse, and a ColBERT-style
# multi-vector — and two of them are unreachable through Ollama, so a whole
# class of retrieval work cannot even be measured while it is the only backend.
# TEI is also what already serves the cross-encoder, so choosing it removes a
# service rather than adding one.
# EMBED_BACKEND=tei
# EMBED_URL=http://localhost:8081
